WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.270
Our garden sensor once logged

2
00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:04.970
eighty-five degrees at six in the morning.

3
00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.320
The tomatoes would have cooked!

4
00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.460
It was a glitch: that sensor says

5
00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.400
eighty-five as it wakes up.

6
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:15.120
Today: a device that remembers,

7
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.340
and which readings to trust.

8
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.720
Today: a device that remembers, with a

9
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.140
time and a unit on every reading.

10
00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:28.070
A data logger is a sensor with a memory:

11
00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:31.730
it reads, stores, waits, and repeats.

12
00:00:32.510 --> 00:00:34.730
The big question is how often.

13
00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.370
Too rarely, and you miss

14
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.370
what happens in between.

15
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.280
Too often, and you fill the

16
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.180
memory and drain the battery.

17
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.600
The rule of thumb: read faster

18
00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.020
than the thing changes.

19
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.480
A rain gauge, once a day.

20
00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.500
A classroom's temperature,

21
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.400
every five or ten minutes.

22
00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.540
A smoke alarm, every few seconds,

23
00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.020
because a fire can't wait.

24
00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:01.890
Quick check!

25
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:05.120
You're logging how fast a

26
00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:06.260
bean plant grows.

27
00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:10.250
Once a day, or every minute?

28
00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:12.370
Once a day.

29
00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.060
A bean plant grows slowly, so a daily

30
00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.000
reading catches every change.

31
00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.140
Every minute gives over a thousand

32
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.160
near-identical numbers a day.

33
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.140
Surprise: every reading is a little wrong.

34
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.300
Read the same room three times, and you

35
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.380
might get twenty-nine and a half,

36
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.800
thirty and a half, then thirty.

37
00:01:36.370 --> 00:01:39.330
That wobble is called noise, and

38
00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:40.970
cheap sensors have more of it.

39
00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.980
To calm the noise, average the last few

40
00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:47.320
readings: add them up and divide.

41
00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:49.660
These three average exactly

42
00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.900
thirty, so one jumpy reading can't

43
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:54.380
switch the fan on by mistake.

44
00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.770
But the average reacts later, because

45
00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:59.410
it remembers older readings.

46
00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:02.310
Averaging has a weak spot.

47
00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:05.320
Mix our eighty-five degree glitch with

48
00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.740
two real readings of thirty, and the

49
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.620
average jumps above forty-eight.

50
00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:11.580
The middle reading, the

51
00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.200
median, stays at thirty.

52
00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:16.310
So remove glitches first, and

53
00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.490
write down what you removed.

54
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.100
A memory is only useful if

55
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:22.360
it makes sense later.

56
00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.240
So every reading gets a time, a unit and a

57
00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:30.740
place: Monday at ten fifteen, thirty

58
00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.840
degrees Celsius, the science room.

59
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.080
Without a unit, thirty

60
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:36.000
could mean anything.

61
00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:37.870
Your turn.

62
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.860
A gas alarm averages its last ten

63
00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:44.600
minutes of readings, to hide the noise.

64
00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:46.150
Good idea?

65
00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:50.870
No: a gas alarm must react at once.

66
00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:54.790
Remember: read as often as the job needs.

67
00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:57.980
Every reading wobbles; averaging

68
00:02:57.980 --> 00:02:59.920
calms it but reacts later.

69
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.260
And give every reading a

70
00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.640
time, a unit and a place.

71
00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:07.770
Try it this week: be a

72
00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.030
data logger for a day.

73
00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:12.510
Every hour, read a thermometer, or

74
00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.370
measure a stick's shadow in the sun.

75
00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.870
Write the time, the reading and the unit

76
00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.190
in a table, then look for the pattern.
